# Spring Boot REST API with Path Variables

## Basic Path Variable Example
```java
@RestController
public class StudentController {

    // Spring Boot REST API with Path Variable
    @GetMapping("/students/{id}")
    public Student studentPathVariable(@PathVariable int id) {
        // {id} is URI template variable
        // Example URL: http://localhost:8080/students/1
        return new Student(id, "Ramesh", "Fadatare");
    }
}
```

## Multiple Path Variables Example
```java
@GetMapping("/students/{id}/{first-name}/{last-name}")
public Student studentPathVariable(
        @PathVariable int id,
        @PathVariable("first-name") String firstName,
        @PathVariable("last-name") String lastName) {
    return new Student(id, firstName, lastName);
}
```

## How @PathVariable Works
- @PathVariable binds URI template variables to method parameters
- URI template variables are defined in curly braces in the URL path: {variable-name}
- Example URLs:
  - Single variable: http://localhost:8080/students/1
  - Multiple variables: http://localhost:8080/students/1/admin/administrator

## Important Notes
1. When variable names match:
   - If method parameter name matches URI template variable name, @PathVariable doesn't need the name specified
   ```java
   @GetMapping("/students/{id}")
   public Student get(@PathVariable int id) // Works fine
   ```

2. When variable names don't match:
   - Must specify the URI template variable name in @PathVariable
   ```java
   @GetMapping("/students/{id}")
   public Student get(@PathVariable("id") int studentId) // Must specify "id"
   ```

3. Naming Conventions:
   - For multi-word variables in URL, use hyphen-case: {first-name}
   - In Java parameters, use camelCase: firstName

## Response Format
```json
{
    "id": 1,
    "firstName": "admin",
    "lastName": "administrator"
}
```

## Key Points
- @PathVariable is used to handle dynamic values in the URL path
- Multiple path variables can be used in a single endpoint
- Path variables are part of the URL path, making them suitable for RESTful resource identification
- Spring Boot automatically handles type conversion of path variables to method parameter types

